  <h2 id="resumen">Executive summary</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p">
    Bodega Digital is a smart tool for managing wine inventory in wineries and
    companies that handle large numbers of bottles. The project, funded by the
    Ministry of Industry, Trade and Tourism and by the European NextGenerationEU
    fund, was planned in two phases: radio-frequency identification (RFID) of
    bottles with demand forecasting, and inventory refinement through computer
    vision.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-2">
    Quabu Solutions took part as the consortium's technology partner, contributing
    software development and integration. My work was the architecture and the
    domain model, on a monorepo monolith with frontend, backend and database in the
    same repository, with DDD for boundaries and TDD for business logic.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-3">
    It was built in two phases —March to July 2024 and October 2024 to February
    2025—, separated by the development of Quabu's Atlassian Addons. One deployment
    and one integration cycle for both phases.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contexto">Context: a winery is not just any warehouse</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-4">
    In a winery, bottles can spend up to fifteen years ageing, with different
    operations during that time. And references look very similar to each other:
    the year, the variety, the plot change. Knowing how many bottles there are, of
    which one and where they are is a real business problem, not an inventory
    detail.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-5">
    The project was born for the wine sector, with the idea of later expanding to
    other areas —manufacturing, airports, agrifood or medicine— where identifying
    individual units inside a stock also matters.
  </p>

  <h2 id="problema">The problem</h2>
  <ul id="bodega-digital-ul">
    <li id="bodega-digital-li">
      Identify each bottle individually, not by reference: vintage, variety and plot
      make every unit a different case.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-2">
      Read those labels in an environment hostile to radio frequency: the liquid in
      the bottle alters readings, and a full shelf concentrates hundreds of tags.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-3">
      Anticipate demand to avoid stock-outs and improve availability for the end
      customer.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-4">
      Maintain traceability across the entire wine value chain.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="restricciones">Constraints</h2>
  <ul id="bodega-digital-ul-2">
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-5">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong">RFID over liquid.</strong> UHF tags had to be evaluated for the bottle
      neck while minimising read errors.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-6">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong-2">High density.</strong> Antennas had to work with many bottles
      together, not with one isolated label.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-7">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong-3">Consortium project.</strong> Six partners —Clúster Digital de
      Catalunya, INNOVI, Grupo ICA, Raw Data, DO5 Hispanobodegas and Quabu
      Solutions— with public funding and committed phases.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-8">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong-4">Two phases.</strong> The first with RFID and demand forecasting; the
      second with computer vision to refine inventory.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rol">My role</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-6">
    My work focused on architecture and the domain model: which pieces existed,
    where the boundaries were and how business logic was tested. I was part of a
    team and a consortium: what follows distinguishes what I designed from what was
    collective work.
  </p>
  <ul id="bodega-digital-ul-3">
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-9">
      I designed the monorepo monolith: frontend, backend and database in the same
      repository, with a single deployment.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-10">
      I modelled the domain with DDD: bottle, reference, vintage, batch and position
      as vocabulary shared with the business.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-11">
      I supported the logic with TDD, so inventory rules could change without
      breaking the ones that already worked.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-12">
      I placed each integration —RFID readers, computer vision, the prediction
      model— behind its own adapter.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="arquitectura">Architecture: a monorepo monolith</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-7">
    Three pieces in one repository: frontend, backend and database. One deployment,
    one integration cycle and the client-server contract versioned alongside, so an
    API change was visible in the same place as its consumer.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-8">
    A monolith does not mean an unstructured block. Separation happened inside: use
    cases, layers and domain boundaries. The infrastructure was one, but the code
    was not one big pile.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-9">
    That is where I brought backend architecture into the frontend for the first
    time: the same DDD logic layer used by the server, combined with Atomic Design
    in the interface.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-tdd">DDD and TDD inside the monolith</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-10">
    DDD provided the language and boundaries. The code used the winery's vocabulary
    —bottles, references, vintages, batches, positions— instead of talking about
    tables and forms, and each context could change without dragging the others.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-11">
    TDD provided the safety net. Inventory rules were written as tests before
    implementation, so the model could change without fear of breaking a rule
    nobody remembered. In a monolith that net is especially profitable: the whole
    domain is tested in one process, without standing up distributed infrastructure
    to check it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="integraciones">RFID, vision and forecasting</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-12">
    The first phase combined radio-frequency identification and machine-learning
    demand forecasting. The second added computer vision to collect additional
    information and improve inventory accuracy. In advanced phases, the project
    planned to integrate Blockchain alongside RFID to provide full traceability
    across the value chain.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-13">
    Each of those pieces enters through an adapter. The domain expresses what it
    needs —identify a bottle, know its position, anticipate a stock-out— and the
    adapter handles how a reader, a camera or a model solves it. Changing hardware
    or model does not force changes to business rules.
  </p>

  <h2 id="evolucion">What remained of the monolith</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-14">
    The monolith lasted through both product phases: one deployment, one integration
    cycle and the domain in one repository. What survived the project was the domain
    model, the language shared with operations and the way of testing.
  </p>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-15">
    <a id="bodega-digital-a" href="/en/projects/nakire-tps/">Nakire TPS</a> is an in-house product, with
    services and events. What it takes from here is the way of working: the monolith
    was not a shortcut that had to be undone, it was the place where the domain was
    understood well enough for the model to serve later in another system.
  </p>

  <h2 id="decisiones">Technical decisions</h2>
  <ul id="bodega-digital-ul-4">
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-13">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong-5">Monorepo monolith instead of services.</strong> One deployment and one
      integration cycle, in exchange for assuming a single scaling rhythm.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-14">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong-6">DDD inside the monolith.</strong> Domain boundaries in the code, in
      exchange for modelling effort that is only recovered if the domain is
      respected.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-15">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong-7">TDD over business logic.</strong> Tests first in the domain, in
      exchange for writing more code than strictly needed to start.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-16">
      <strong id="bodega-digital-strong-8">One adapter per integration.</strong> RFID, vision and forecasting
      change at their own pace, in exchange for keeping the port contract stable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="resultados">Outcomes</h2>
  <p id="bodega-digital-p-16">
    The outcomes I can state are the ones the project declares, not invented
    metrics:
  </p>
  <ul id="bodega-digital-ul-5">
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-17">
      Each bottle is identified individually, with its vintage, variety and plot.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-18">
      Product availability is queried quickly and in detail, instead of by estimate.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-19">
      Demand forecasting makes it possible to anticipate stock-outs.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-20">
      Traceability follows the bottle along the value chain.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="aprendizajes">Lessons learned</h2>
  <ul id="bodega-digital-ul-6">
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-21">
      Separation is decided inside the code before it is decided in infrastructure:
      a monolith with clear boundaries can be split later; one without them cannot.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-22">
      DDD and TDD are not distributed-architecture ceremony: they support a product
      that still fits in one deployment just as well.
    </li>
    <li id="bodega-digital-li-23">
      In projects with public funding and several partners, shared vocabulary is
      part of the delivery.
    </li>
  </ul>
